  <static seed="5" source="Contrib/PCC/BasicAlgebra/Exponents/ExponentDefinition40.pg">
    <statement><p>Evaluate the following.
<ol label="a.">
<li>
<p><m>(-2)^{3}</m></p>
<p><fillin characters="8" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>-4^{3}</m></p>
<p><fillin characters="8" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-8</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-64</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>Remember to use order of operations here: parentheses take priority over exponents, and exponents take priority over multiplication.</p>
<p>The quantities evaluate to:</p>
<p><ol label="a.">
<li>
<p><m>\begin{aligned}[t]
              (-2)^{3}\amp =(-2)\cdot(-2)\cdot(-2)\\
                         \amp  ={-8}  
          \end{aligned}</m></p>
<p>When we multiply three negative numbers, the result is negative. Two negative signs cancelled, but one is left, making the result negative.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\begin{aligned}[t]
            -4^{3}\amp =-1\cdot4^3\\
                    \amp =-1\cdot4\cdot4\cdot4\\
                    \amp  = -1\cdot 64\\
                    \amp ={-64}
           \end{aligned}</m></p>
<p>Note that the negative sign is like <lq/>negative one multiplied by....<rq/>. For example:</p>
<p><ul label="square">
<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle{-2=-1\cdot2}</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle{-3=-1\cdot3}</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle{-4=-1\cdot4}</m></p>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p>You can see the pattern. This demonstrates that the negative sign can be viewed as multiplication by <m>-1</m>.</p>
<p>So, we can change <m>-4^{3}</m> into <m>-1\cdot4^{2}</m>. Since exponents take priority over multiplication, we evaluate <m>4^2</m> <em>before</em> multiplying by <m>-1</m>.</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</solution>
